ln ---> hardlink 

ln creates another filename that points to same inode and same file data.Renaming or deleting one hard-link name does not affect the other as long as at least one hard link remains

Synatx : ln original_file hardlink_name

Example : ln testfile testfile-hardlink


ln -s --> symlink(Symbolic/softlink)

ln -s creates a symbolic link that points to the path of another file or directory.

> different inode number
> if we rename original file of a symlink , the symlink usually broken because it still point old path

Syntax : ln -s source_file filename








